92186045585 true] [17592186399488 1084 #uuid "290c0a9d-69e6-58cd-ba0d-a2fb19aa48f8" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399489 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399490 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399489 1069 "q-127770" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399489 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399489 1071 "Consider a game of chance with $4$ prizes ($$1$, $$2$, $$3$, and $$4$) where each outcome has a specific probability of happening, shown in the table below. \n\n| $\\text{Number}$ | $\\text{Probability}$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $$1$ | $40%$ |\n| $$2$ | $30%$ |\n| $$3$ | $20%$ |\n| $$4$ | $10%$ |\n\nWhat is the expected profit per game?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399489 1072 #uuid "150d488e-5edb-5db7-b1b9-30f4afcb814f" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399489 1074 "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single game. Using the given probabilities, let's summarize this in a table.  If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ | $\\frac{3}{10}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $p$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[p] & =1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{2}{5})+2(\\frac{3}{10})+3(\\frac{1}{5})+4(\\frac{1}{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{10}+\\frac{6}{10}+\\frac{6}{10}+\\frac{4}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{10} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $$2.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399490 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399491 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399490 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399492 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399490 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399490 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399494 13194139533478 17592186045585 tru