1081 "$B$ and $C$ only" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399500 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399500 1084 #uuid "61887468-5253-5e21-a263-8a4203dd1713" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399501 1081 "$A$ and $B$ only" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399501 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399501 1084 #uuid "bdb370b3-9b49-53aa-adf8-a245cfd5dfd0" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399502 1081 "$B$ only" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399502 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399502 1084 #uuid "1a4d2564-d98c-55bf-bdf9-5d34fdcf8601" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399503 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399504 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399503 1069 "q-128139" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399503 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399503 1071 "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If a $2\\times 2$-matrix $A$ is invertible, then $\\text{__________}$ . *\n\nI. $det(A)\\ne 0$\nII. $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution\nIII. $Ax=0$ has infinitely many solutions" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399503 1072 #uuid "a2d9404d-2d42-56c6-b91b-5351ccec57cf" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399503 1074 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNow, since the first part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($A$ is invertible), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only options I and III give us true statements:\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $det(A)\\ne 0.$* $\\,✓$\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$* $\\,✓$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399504 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399505 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399504 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399506 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399504 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399507 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399504 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399508 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399504 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399509 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399504 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399504 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399504 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399508 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399504 1080 #uuid "9e5bc233-a857-5531-b1c0-5d6872ab74e0" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399505 1081 "II and III only" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399505 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399505 1084 #uuid "32703bd1-510a-5493-8fea-27ee4c97cf9e" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399506 1081 "I and III only" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399506 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399506 1084 #uuid "99ce61c4-029a-53a5-a2e3-716c725f41b9" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399507 1081 "III only" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399507 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399507 1084 #uuid "f6430495-e643-5b57-a059-f4ae97b5f229" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399508 1081 "I and II only" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399508 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399508 1084 #uuid "b8a6703f-ba79-5105-aa0e-79c1dad1aa0d" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399509 1081 "II only" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399509 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399509 1084 #uuid "0dd7aae6-48b9-58c3-b530-c8d20775ab70" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399510 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399511 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399510 1069 "q-128461" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399510 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399510 1071 "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=⟨{k}^{2},7,4⟩,$ $b=⟨6,7,4⟩,$ and $c=⟨3,0,2⟩$ are coplanar." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399510 1072 #uuid "41e53fe4-b06e-5150-a976-5badf29f5d4c" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399510 1074 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2} & 7 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 7 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={k}^{2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|-7\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|+4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}6 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={k}^{2}(7\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 4)-7(6\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 4)+4(6\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 7) \\\\  & =14{k}^{2}+0-84 \\\\  & =14{k}^{2}-84 \\\\  & =14({k}^{2}-6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =0 \\\\ 14({k}^{2}-6) & =0 \\\\ {k}^{2}-6 & =0 \\\\ {k}^{2} & =6 \\\\ k & =\\pm \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\sqrt{6}$ or $k=\\sqrt{6}.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399511 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399512 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399511 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399513 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399511 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399514 1